Data Structures: Structs vs Dicts

In Python, you use Dictionaries to group named data:

d = {'volts': [1, 2], 'unit': 'V'}

In MATLAB, use Structures (struct):

data.volts = [1 2];
data.unit = 'V';

Accessed using dot notation: value = data.volts(1);

Data Structures: Cell Arrays vs Lists

In Python, Lists can hold anything: L = [1, 'text', np.array([1])].
In MATLAB, standard arrays must be homogeneous (all numbers).
Use Cell Arrays (curly braces {}) to store mixed types:

C = {1, 'text', [1 2 3]};
Access content with curly braces: val = C{1};

